WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:04.130
Film Courage: You mentioned a conversation
that you had about work and how it’s good

00:00:04.130 --> 00:00:06.920
work and how it’s good work or bad work
is never really finished.

00:00:06.920 --> 00:00:14.650
We are in this new age of these sort of gurus
and these success articles on five tips to

00:00:14.650 --> 00:00:16.860
make you more productive whatever.

00:00:16.860 --> 00:00:20.530
So something David was talking about was a
sustained obsession.

00:00:20.530 --> 00:00:24.710
He said that he’d read and heard from so
many people that most successful people are

00:00:24.710 --> 00:00:27.100
just obsessed with their work, they are workaholics.

00:00:27.100 --> 00:00:28.290
Do you think that’s true?

00:00:28.290 --> 00:00:31.640
Dr. Ken Atchity, Author/Producer: There’s
a lot of truth in it and I’ve gone through

00:00:31.640 --> 00:00:38.270
that same thought process especially when
writing a book about the creative mind.

00:00:38.270 --> 00:00:41.850
I don’t think that workaholics is the right
word for it.

00:00:41.850 --> 00:00:49.390
I call it The Type C Personality because workaholics
comes from the Selye’s concept of the Type

00:00:49.390 --> 00:00:55.360
A and Type B personality and from what I can
make out the Type A personality is the unhappy

00:00:55.360 --> 00:01:01.550
workaholic who can’t do anything in life
except work and who basically is making himself

00:01:01.550 --> 00:01:06.979
and the people around him miserable because
of his work and his obsessive need to be working

00:01:06.979 --> 00:01:08.270
all of the time.

00:01:08.270 --> 00:01:12.920
And then there’s the Type P personality
who is never very well-defined and who somehow

00:01:12.920 --> 00:01:20.560
doesn’t become an ideal as Selye describes
it but somebody who is well-adjusted and doesn’t

00:01:20.560 --> 00:01:26.659
feel the same crazy pressures that the Type
A feels and I thought well the problem with

00:01:26.659 --> 00:01:32.100
that theory is that it leaves out people who
absolutely love their work and who are able

00:01:32.100 --> 00:01:41.029
to live other full lives at the same time
and I started thinking about that and realizing

00:01:41.029 --> 00:01:46.049
their are a lot of people like that and I
call them Type C’s in one of my books and

00:01:46.049 --> 00:01:52.319
the Type C is the creative personality that
loves to work, would probably rather be working

00:01:52.319 --> 00:01:57.709
than anything else but isn’t negatively
impacted by that at all.

00:01:57.709 --> 00:01:59.619
Instead they just thrive on their work.

00:01:59.619 --> 00:02:07.809
I mean there’s an example I saw long ago
as Pablo Casals the great cellist was so crippled

00:02:07.809 --> 00:02:16.430
with arthritis when he got older that he had
to be carried from his bed every morning to

00:02:16.430 --> 00:02:22.489
his piano bench because he warmed up every
day by playing the piano for half of an hour

00:02:22.489 --> 00:02:23.530
or an hour.

00:02:23.530 --> 00:02:25.970
But he got to the point where he couldn’t
walk to the bench.

00:02:25.970 --> 00:02:32.670
He had to be carried to the bench and then
stacked on to the bench and his arms had to

00:02:32.670 --> 00:02:39.000
be lifted onto the keyboard and then he would
slowly but surely start playing for an hour.

00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:47.769
And at the end of the hour he walked to the
kitchen for breakfast and he did this everyday

00:02:47.769 --> 00:02:56.420
for his last ten years and he was reactivating
his body through creative process that he

00:02:56.420 --> 00:02:58.870
was very well in tune with.

00:02:58.870 --> 00:03:06.609
And I realize that Type C personalities are
people who have this creative affliction or

00:03:06.609 --> 00:03:13.620
whatever you call it (this gift) but understand
it as opposed to those who don’t understand

00:03:13.620 --> 00:03:19.590
it who have often tragic endings like Sylvia
Plath or Hemingway or Virginia Wolf or many

00:03:19.590 --> 00:03:24.080
others in the creative world who never understand
their process.

00:03:24.080 --> 00:03:29.319
Who think that every time they finish a book
it’s the end of the world and they go into

00:03:29.319 --> 00:03:31.310
a deep depression.

00:03:31.310 --> 00:03:36.370
This is very common in the creative world
is to be depressed after you finish the work.

00:03:36.370 --> 00:03:42.489
So when you really think about that the solution
is obvious never get to the point where you

00:03:42.489 --> 00:03:47.821
are finishing the work and that happens on
the negative side for a lot of people as well

00:03:47.821 --> 00:03:52.579
who can never finish their book or never finish
their article or never finish their poem because

00:03:52.579 --> 00:03:56.280
they are afraid of finishing, fear of finishing.

00:03:56.280 --> 00:04:02.209
And as a tenured professor I was always on
committees judging other people who couldn’t

00:04:02.209 --> 00:04:03.639
finish anything.

00:04:03.639 --> 00:04:08.599
And one of my colleagues who like me had published
many books we were both on the same committee

00:04:08.599 --> 00:04:12.719
judging another colleague who had not finished
a single book.

00:04:12.719 --> 00:04:20.419
And my colleague said to me “You know you
and I would write a book in the time it takes

00:04:20.419 --> 00:04:25.750
him to research a chapter in a book.”

00:04:25.750 --> 00:04:29.900
And I said “Yes, because I always do my
research last.”

00:04:29.900 --> 00:04:34.270
I write the book first and then do the research.

00:04:34.270 --> 00:04:40.050
So I already know it’s going to end, but
to get back to this idea of finishing.

00:04:40.050 --> 00:04:47.280
A simple solution to this postpartum depression
is when you know when you are almost done

00:04:47.280 --> 00:04:53.600
whether you are in a home stretch of a book
or a screenplay or whatever it is.

00:04:53.600 --> 00:04:57.800
Stop, take a day off, take two days off.

00:04:57.800 --> 00:05:02.800
Because the energy of finishing is so huge
that it will easily be recalled when you sit

00:05:02.800 --> 00:05:07.370
down again when you allow it into this compartment
that you are using.

00:05:07.370 --> 00:05:16.020
But take a day off 
because every creative person has another

00:05:16.020 --> 00:05:18.669
project that is dying to be next.

00:05:18.669 --> 00:05:24.740
You can sit down and start it and do it to
a point where you can’t wait to go on with

00:05:24.740 --> 00:05:29.800
it and then stop and go back and finish you
were finishing.

00:05:29.800 --> 00:05:34.580
And then you will discover that there is no
more postpartum depression because you haven’t

00:05:34.580 --> 00:05:40.870
allowed it, you simply managed the time, the
finite commodity at your disposal.

00:05:40.870 --> 00:05:44.920
You’ve managed the time so you don’t have
to deal with that.

00:05:44.920 --> 00:05:50.690
Because being depressed is most of the time
basically a waste most of the time for an

00:05:50.690 --> 00:05:51.690
artist.

00:05:51.690 --> 00:05:57.090
You can allow it for awhile if it gives you
great ideas and depends your pathos and the

00:05:57.090 --> 00:06:02.990
things you need to draw on but basically too
much of it is a waste of time.

00:06:02.990 --> 00:06:08.650
You know one of my mentors years ago John
Gardner (the novelist) said “People should

00:06:08.650 --> 00:06:10.550
just start doing more.

00:06:10.550 --> 00:06:13.400
It gets rid of all the moods they’re having.”

00:06:13.400 --> 00:06:19.530
If you are in a down mood, get up and run
around the block and literally that works.

00:06:19.530 --> 00:06:24.440
I mean if you get your body going and run
around the block it’s hard to be in that

00:06:24.440 --> 00:06:28.690
kind of morbid, depressed state you are in
before.

00:06:28.690 --> 00:06:35.610
So managing your moods like that is what separates
a productive, creative happy person from a

00:06:35.610 --> 00:06:39.910
productive unhappy person.

00:06:39.910 --> 00:06:42.319
You notice I’m not talking about the unproductive
ones.

00:06:42.319 --> 00:06:43.669
That’s a whole different subject.

00:06:43.669 --> 00:06:48.909
But I’m talking about people who are creatively
productive and have careers, they are still

00:06:48.909 --> 00:06:55.099
divided into unhappy ones and the happy ones
and it’s a matter of understanding how your

00:06:55.099 --> 00:07:00.520
mind is working that makes you part of the
happy group and you don’t have to be part

00:07:00.520 --> 00:07:06.720
of the unhappy group despite a lot of urban
myths to the contrary that basically say the

00:07:06.720 --> 00:07:10.210
artist has to be suffering and tortured and
all of that.

00:07:10.210 --> 00:07:11.970
It’s really not necessary.

00:07:11.970 --> 00:07:14.470
Film Courage: Well you get more attention
that way.

00:07:14.470 --> 00:07:16.879
I know Julia Cameron [Author of The Artist’s
Way, Vein of Gold and more] talks about (and

00:07:16.879 --> 00:07:22.960
I’m butchering this I’m sure) that the
unhappy blocked artist gets a lot of attention,

00:07:22.960 --> 00:07:29.419
pats on the back and friends around them where
the productive, happier person finds themselves

00:07:29.419 --> 00:07:34.699
sometimes alone because it’s a little threatening
or it’s just hard to…

00:07:34.699 --> 00:07:42.810
Ken: Yes…but being alone is wonderful so…the
happy productive artist loves being alone

00:07:42.810 --> 00:07:47.760
and he loves being with people too but he
loves being alone because it’s truly when

00:07:47.760 --> 00:07:54.909
he is under the most command of all of his
powers and facilities when nothing can interrupt

00:07:54.909 --> 00:07:59.520
him and he’s focused on the work, that’s
great.

00:07:59.520 --> 00:08:03.830
And getting too much sympathy, as a literary
manager who has managed hundreds of managers

00:08:03.830 --> 00:08:14.160
over my career I think the ones that are unhappy
and looking for attention, you really get

00:08:14.160 --> 00:08:17.439
tired of them fast if you are dealing with
them all of the time.

00:08:17.439 --> 00:08:22.280
If they are in your family and you only have
to see them once a week, okay fine.

00:08:22.280 --> 00:08:26.800
If you only have to see them at Thanksgiving,
okay even better.

00:08:26.800 --> 00:08:32.260
But if you are somebody who deals with them
everyday sooner or later the ones that are

00:08:32.260 --> 00:08:38.210
constantly complaining go onto your Life Is
Too Short list and those are not the ones

00:08:38.210 --> 00:08:39.760
you are looking for.

00:08:39.760 --> 00:08:47.560
And they are what I call pseudo artists who
end up not being productive most of the time.

00:08:47.560 --> 00:08:52.060
Who are longing to be artist but don’t have
the mental discipline to actually do it.

